:design

# Live Sync Engine — proven chat-first into empty prod (≤1GB fly machine)

The greenfield Studio v2 (`studio-v2.work`) names a **live-shapes sync layer** as the fix for the
slow dashboard, but P2 (live-shapes sync) was never shipped — the dashboard still runs SSE +
per-view HTTP fetch. This is the goal that closes it, chat-first, ending in a published density
number from a real production machine. Epic: **wb-d8ac** (studio v2). Tracked in bd.

## North star

A real client subscribes to a `Nexus.Shapes` channel over WS against a **live nexus in production**,
sends a chat message, and every other subscriber sees the delta — snapshot + insert/update/delete —
with tenant isolation intact, all on **one 1GB fly machine**. Density is the score: max concurrent
{workspaces × channels × subscribers} synced under 1GB before degradation. Chat is the beachhead;
every other dashboard surface (runs, inbox, usage, storage, data) then rides the *same* shape transport.

## Goals

- **G1 — E2E chat round-trip (the missing test).** `nexus/test/live_chat_sync_test.exs`: real Bandit,
  multi-tenant Cloud auth, raw RFC-6455 sockets. Two same-org clients subscribe `{op:"shape",
  name:"msgs",scope:channel}`; `Store.create(Message)` → both get `shape:init` then `shape:delta`. A
  cross-tenant client gets **nothing**. Covers insert / update (edit) / delete / reaction / read-cursor.
  This is the contract the rest builds on. **DONE.**

- **G2 — Wire dashboard Studio to live-shapes.** Replace Studio's HTTP-fetch + SSE-inbox with a
  `liveShapes(resource, filter)` client (`desktop/src/lib/rcp/client.ts` `socket()` exists). Studio
  messages render from `shape:init` and mutate from deltas — no re-fetch. Delete the per-view SWR churn.

- **G3 — Presence + typing on the same socket.** Wire `{op:"presence"}` into Studio off `Nexus.Presence`
  (already TTL-swept). Proves one multiplexed socket carries durable shapes + ephemeral presence.

- **G4 — Generalize: one transport, every surface.** Promote `liveShapes` to the dashboard's single
  data primitive. Re-point runs, inbox, usage, storage onto shape subscriptions (replacing the 5
  `Nexus.Live` SSE sources). One sync engine, many filters. Inbox events post to owning channel.

- **G5 — Empty-prod density harness.** Deploy current runtime to a fresh, empty 1GB fly machine (own
  app, not wb-dogfood). `scripts/sync-density.exs` opens N WS clients via the prod handshake, fans M
  msgs/sec across K channels, measures delivered-delta latency p50/p99, dropped deltas, RSS. Report the
  ceiling: "1GB holds X subscribers across Y channels at Z msg/s, p99 < N ms." That number = full faith.

- **G6 — Reconnect + catch-up.** Client drops, reconnects, replays missed deltas (cursor/since-ts on
  subscribe). No gaps, no dupes after a kill-9 mid-stream. Without this, "live" is a demo, not an engine.

## Done

G1 green in CI · Studio fully live (no fetch-on-render) · all surfaces on shape transport · published
density number from a real prod 1GB machine · reconnect-safe.

## G5 — engine density (local, the floor)

`nexus/scripts/sync-density.exs` drives the core shape fan-out engine (N subscribers across K
channels, R rounds). A subscriber here = one `Nexus.Shapes.subscribe` process, exactly what each
`/ws` socket holds server-side. Measured locally:

| subscribers | channels | VM memory | dropped | p50 | p99 |
| ----------- | -------- | --------- | ------- | ----- | ---- |
| 2,000       | 20       | 122 MB    | 0       | 0.22ms | 0.52ms |
| 20,000      | 100      | 302 MB    | 0       | 1.4ms | 3.9ms |
| 50,000      | 200      | 343 MB    | 0       | 5.8ms | 11ms |

**Finding: the fan-out engine is NOT the Starter-tier (1GB) bottleneck** — 50k live subscriptions sit
in ~343MB with zero dropped deltas. ~2.8–6.4 KB per live subscription. The real ceiling is the WS
transport layer (a socket process + buffers + TLS per subscriber) which rides ON this engine — that is
the next measurement (the WS-socket harness reuses these same percentiles), and the one that runs
against the real Starter-tier fly machine for the published "full faith" number.

## G2 — what shipped (backend)

The new model (user decision): channel chat IS the model — durable `Message` rows via live-shapes
replace the old agent-session chat.

- `Message`/`Reaction` gain a server-assigned `id :id` — the stable key threads (`parent=id`) and
  insert/update/delete deltas reconcile on.
- `POST /cloud/chat/send` — derives author from the authed identity, assigns id+ts,
  `Store.create(Message)` → live-shapes `:insert` delta fan-out.
- e2e test: real HTTP send → WS subscriber gets the delta with the server id; cross-org isolated.
- Client UI wiring held pending the user's broader new-model redesign (the old agent-session Studio
  surface is being replaced).

## G1 — what shipped

- `Nexus.Store.delete/3` + a `:delete` live-shape delta (ETS native `delete/3`; generic fallback).
- `Nexus.Store.update/4` now emits a **full post-image** per-row `:update` delta (the coarse
  `match ∪ changes` map dropped the scope field, so scoped subscribers never saw edits).
- The cloud workbook's `server.register/0` registers the chat shapes (`msgs`→{Message,:channel},
  `reactions`→{Reaction,:message}, `cursors`→{Cursor,:channel}) — OUR opinion in OUR workbook (THE LINE).
- `nexus/test/live_chat_sync_test.exs` — real-socket e2e with a zero-dependency gen_tcp WS client.
